From 6537e3b998d6654e9bf8d229d7c0f2f84ce38eb9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jackwang0009 <93422958+jackwang0009@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 13:56:29 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] [upstream-candidate] Write a text with the font resource it
 was parsed with

The generator looked up the resource name of a font by base font name
and font type (CPDF_PageObjectHolder::FontsMapSearch()). The first font
of a kind got a new resource, FXF<n>, and every later font with the
same base font name and type was written with that name as well. Fonts
that share a base font name are not the same font: they differ in
encoding, widths, CMap or font program. The texts of all but the first
one came out with the wrong glyphs:

- two Helvetica fonts, one of them with /Differences in its encoding;
- two Type0 fonts over one CIDFont name, with different CMaps
  (Identity-H and UniGB-UCS2-H);
- three Type0 fonts, each with a font program of its own, whose
  descendant fonts carry the same /BaseFont (that is where PDFium takes
  the base font name of a Type0 font from).

A font dictionary that was a direct object of the resources was taken
for a standard font and replaced by a dictionary with nothing but
/Type, /Subtype, /BaseFont and /Encoding.

Look the font dictionary up in the /Font resources of the holder
instead, preferring the name the text was parsed with, the same way
shadings and graphics state parameter dictionaries are looked up. A
font dictionary that is an indirect object and not a resource of the
holder yet is added under a new name. Only a font whose dictionary is
neither (a stock font, as before; a direct object of the resources of
another page, as before) still goes by base font name and type.

Fonts keep their resource names this way, so two expectations of
FPDFEditEmbedderTest change: after a text is removed, the font of the
text that is left is still called /F2 instead of /FXF1.

Tests: CPDFPageContentGeneratorTest.ProcessFontsWithSameBaseFont.
pdfium_unittests and pdfium_embeddertests: no new failures.

Verification (ops_diff.py): differences in fonts 4 -> 0 on the public
test files (bug_781804.pdf, hello_world_2_pages_split_streams.pdf).
No-diff pages 65/69 -> 67/69 synthetic (text-standard14,
text-cid-cjk-nonembedded), 384/388 -> 385/388 public
(hello_world_2_pages_split_streams.pdf page 2: its content streams are
shared with page 1 and still rewritten along with page 1, which no
longer shows because the names in them stay valid for page 2).

Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>
